,"parts":[{"text":"ஆயின்","lemma":"āyiṉ","gloss":"if it is"},{"text":"உம்","lemma":"um","gloss":"even"}],"gloss":"even if"},{"surface":"படு","parts":[{"text":"படு","lemma":"paṭu","gloss":"obtained"}],"gloss":"obtained"},{"surface":"பதம்","parts":[{"text":"பதம்","lemma":"patam","gloss":"food"}],"gloss":"food"},{"surface":"பிழை","parts":[{"text":"பிழை","lemma":"piḻai","gloss":"to fail"}],"gloss":"failing"},{"surface":"யாது","parts":[{"text":"யாது","lemma":"yātu","gloss":"not"}],"gloss":"without"}]},{"words":[{"surface":"மைஊன்","parts":[{"text":"மைஊன்","lemma":"மைஊன்","gloss":""}],"gloss":"","split_unverified":true},{"surface":"மொசித்த","parts":[{"text":"மொசித்த","lemma":"மொசித்த","gloss":""}],"gloss":"","split_unverified":true},{"surface":"ஒக்கலொடு","parts":[{"text":"ஒக்கலொடு","lemma":"ஒக்கலொடு","gloss":""}],"gloss":"","split_unverified":true},{"surface":"துறை","parts":[{"text":"துறை","lemma":"துறை","gloss":""}],"gloss":"","split_unverified":true},{"surface":"நீர்க்","parts":[{"text":"நீர்க்","lemma":"நீர்க்","gloss":""}],"gloss":"","split_unverified":true}],"as_printed":true},{"words":[{"surface":"கைமான்","parts":[{"text":"கை","lemma":"kai","gloss":"hand/trunk"},{"text":"மான்","lemma":"māṉ","gloss":"beast"}],"gloss":"elephant"},{"surface":"கொள்ளு","parts":[{"text":"கொள்","lemma":"koḷ","gloss":"to take"},{"text":"உ","lemma":"u","gloss":"verbal participle suffix"}],"gloss":"taking"},{"surface":"மோ","parts":[{"text":"மோ","lemma":"mō","gloss":"interrogative particle"}],"gloss":"will it?"},{"surface":"என","parts":[{"text":"என்","lemma":"eṉ","gloss":"to say"},{"text":"அ","lemma":"a","gloss":"infinitive suffix"}],"gloss":"saying"}]},{"words":[{"surface":"உறையுள்","parts":[{"text":"உறை","lemma":"uṟai","gloss":"to dwell"},{"text":"உள்","lemma":"uḷ","gloss":"inside/place"}],"gloss":"dwelling place"},{"surface":"முனியும்","parts":[{"text":"முனி","lemma":"muṉi","gloss":"to dislike"},{"text":"உம்","lemma":"um","gloss":"habitual future suffix"}],"gloss":"will dislike"},{"surface":"அவன்","parts":[{"text":"அவன்","lemma":"avaṉ","gloss":"he"}],"gloss":"he"},{"surface":"செல்லும்","parts":[{"text":"செல்","lemma":"cel","gloss":"to go"},{"text":"உம்","lemma":"um","gloss":"adjectival participle suffix"}],"gloss":"going"},{"surface":"ஊரே","parts":[{"text":"ஊர்","lemma":"ūr","gloss":"town"},{"text":"ஏ","lemma":"ē","gloss":"emphasis"}],"gloss":"the town indeed"}]}],"literal":"Blooming flower of tumpai, beautiful strong chest, / rounded and long large hands, my lord, for the young man, / two arose indeed, enmities! One indeed, / flower-like kohl-adorned eyes having become pale, shoulders having become thin, / who looked at him, women it bound indeed; one indeed, / festival is not although, obtained food without failing, / black goat meat swarmed with relatives, at the water-entry point, / will the elephant seize? thus saying, / dwelling place will shun, the town he goes to!","translation":"For my young lord, who has a beautiful, strong chest adorned with blooming tumpai flowers and long, rounded, powerful arms, two kinds of enmities have arisen. One is that he has bound the hearts of women who looked upon him, causing their flower-like, kohl-rimmed eyes to grow pale and their shoulders to waste away. The other is that in the towns he marches to, even when there is no festival, his relatives feast abundantly on black goat meat without fail, and the townspeople, fearing that the war elephants might drink up all the water at the river ghats, grow weary of their own homes and abandon them.","modern":"மலர்ந்த தும்பைப் பூவை அணிந்த அழகிய வலிமையான மார்பையும், திரண்ட நீண்ட பெரிய கைகளையும் உடைய என் தலைவனாகிய அந்த இளைஞனுக்கு இரண்டு வகையான பகைகள் தோன்றியுள்ளன. ஒன்று, அவனைக் கண்ட பெண்களின் பூ போன்ற மை தீட்டிய கண்கள் பசலை கொள்ளவும், தோள்கள் மெலியவும் செய்து அவர்களைத் தன்பால் பிணித்துக் கொண்டது. மற்றொன்று, விழாக்கள் இல்லாத நாட்களிலும் அவனது சுற்றத்தார் ஆட்டு இறைச்சியைத் தடையின்றி உண்ணுமாறு அவன் பகைவர் நாடுகளைக் கைப்பற்றுவது; இதனால், நீர் நிலைகளில் அவனது யானைகள் நீரைக் குடித்துவிடுமோ என்று அஞ்சி, மக்கள் தங்கள் வீடுகளை வெறுத்து வெளியேறும் நிலையை அவன் செல்லும் ஊர்கள் அடைகின்றன.","explanation":"The poet Avvaiyār is praising the young prince Pokuṭṭeḻiṉi, the son of Atiyamāṉ. She describes how two distinct 'enmities' or troubles arise wherever he goes. The first is a romantic trouble: the local women who look at him fall deeply in love, causing them to pine away with sallow eyes and thin shoulders. The second is a military trouble: when he invades enemy territories, his large army and relatives feast abundantly on meat, and his massive war elephants crowd the river ghats to drink water, causing the terrified local inhabitants to abandon their homes. The contrast between the soft, pining women and the fierce, invading war elephants highlights the prince's dual nature of irresistible beauty and terrifying military might.","model":"gemini-3.5-flash"}}